{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"homography homography","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","fileName":"Homography.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Homography","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Augmented Reality Computer Vision]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Augmented Reality Computer Vision","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"homography homography","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","fileName":"Homography.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Homography","heading":"Homography","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A homography is a projective transformation that maps points on one plane to their corresponding positions in another image or plane. A square AR marker has known planar coordinates, so its four detected image corners provide enough constraints to estimate this mapping.\n\nWith a calibrated camera, the homography can be decomposed into a rotation and translation consistent with the marker plane. Noise makes the estimate approximate, which is why pose refinement may follow.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Augmented Reality Computer Vision","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"homography homography","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Homography.md","fileName":"Homography.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Homography","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTit